Speed-only engine fixes for the local simulator, applied by sim/bootstrap.sh
to the managed .engine checkout on top of the commit sim/engine.pin names.

Every hunk keeps the gameHash bit-identical -- the six reference seeds
(5000-5005) hash the same with and without this patch.

Passes one through four also keep the observation BYTES identical. The fifth
does not, and is the only part of this patch that does not: with the hook it
adds installed, sprite definitions the reading policy would have discarded on
arrival are no longer sent, so the packet is shorter while the frame index
the policy builds out of it is the same one. That claim is what the six-seed
hash comparison tests, and the fifth-pass section below says how to re-check
the vocabulary itself after a pin move. Off the hook the patch is byte-for-
byte the wire it always was.

Rebased from 1047232f (GV30) onto 63ea0cb7 (GV35). NOTHING WAS ABSORBED
UPSTREAM -- every pass below still had somewhere to land -- but five things
moved or changed shape, and the seventh-pass note at the end says where the
rebase left a cost it did not pay:

- THE MODULE SPLIT (docs/plans/2026-08-01-sim-split.md). `sim.nim` is now
  sim.nim / sim_types.nim / sim_state.nim / sim_config.nim plus the leaf
  modules arena.nim, map_art.nim, rig_art.nim, roster.nim, so every sim-side
  hunk changed file: the types and the SimServer fields to sim_types.nim, the
  asset loaders to rig_art.nim, the map bake's callees to map_art.nim,
  loadCtfMap to arena.nim, teamForSlot/addPlayer to roster.nim. Only the
  addresses moved; not one hunk changed meaning. `memoized` lives in
  sim_types.nim now, which is the one module both the engine's art loaders
  and global.nim can see.
- GV34 GAVE THE VISION CONE A RANGE. `visionRange` is 1.5x the live
  config.gunRange, and computeFovVisible drops a lit cell whose d2 exceeds
  it -- after the bubble test and before the cone test. `FovCone` carries
  `rangeSq` and `keeps` runs that test in exactly upstream's position, so the
  sixth pass's "one expression, two callers" property still holds against the
  rule as it is now.
- GV31 ADDED huge/giant/colossal GENERATED MAP CLASSES, which put FovGridW
  past 255 (colossal is 803 cells wide). The third pass's fog-run width sets
  are `set[uint16]`, not the `set[uint8]` GV30 could afford: a width that
  does not fit is not a slow path, it is a converted-and-wrong def gate.
- GV35 HOISTED THE LABEL VOCABULARY into labels.nim constructors
  (labelSelf, labelWeapon, LabelPrefixLives, LabelSideRight/Left). The
  text-dedup hunks build their labels through those rather than by
  interpolating literals, so labels.nim's rename guard still reaches them --
  a renamed token is a compile error here, not a def that stops deduping.
- GV31 WIDENED THE DAMAGE POP and dropped the `clamp(pop.amount, 1,
  DamagePopMaxAmount)` that used to feed its text. The fifth-pass hunk there
  only hoists the label into a binding, so it follows upstream's expression.

Two facts about the shape of this patch that GV35 made explicit and GV30 did
not. First, sim_types.nim states that SimServer is flatty-serialized
POSITIONALLY into replay keyframes: the five fields this patch adds to it
therefore change that layout, so a keyframe written by a patched engine reads
back only in a patched engine, and the two cache fields ride along in every
keyframe. The local simulator never records a replay, and the managed
checkout is the only thing this patch is applied to. Second, the engine's own
suite is 401 checks at this pin (327 at GV30) and passes patched -- worth
running when a change touches a path the headless hook switches off, because
a six-seed gameHash run never takes those.

First and second pass, in profile order (the GV27 shares these were found at):

- self marker: the outlined self sprite was re-rasterized every frame per
  viewer (~41% of a headless tick) only for addSpriteChanged to dedup the
  send; gate it on the def-exists check. Same for the rotating diamond's
  cached-pixel copy (~14%) -- the diamond emitter has a painted branch
  (stains baked into the stone), so the base-pixel fetch is gated per branch,
  behind the def-exists check for clean stone and behind the label check for
  painted stone.
- sprite-def cache: spriteDefinitionIndex linearly scanned a few hundred
  heap refs and is called from every emitter; keep the cache sorted by
  spriteId and bisect. Nothing reads the cache in insertion order. Every
  insertion site goes through insertDefinition -- there are four at this pin,
  two of them inside GV33's addMapBands, and a site that appends unsorted
  breaks the bisect for every emitter, not just its own.
- object-delete sweep: notin over a seq made it quadratic in the object
  count, which fog runs push into the hundreds; use a HashSet.
- teamForSlot: binding the slot copied its two string fields, and
  slotIdentityIndex fans it out O(order) per identity badge; read in place.
- fog: cache the aim-independent shadowcast per cell (a viewer that only
  turned re-ran it), rebuild the fog run list only when the visibility grid
  changed, and skip the cone sqrt when dot < 0 already decides. fovBlocked is
  not fixed for the game -- a turning diamond restamps the vision mask
  (refreshFovCells) -- so the per-cell shadow cache is invalidated EXACTLY
  when that happens: refreshFovCells records which occlusion values flipped,
  and applyDiamondGeometry drops precisely the cached shadows (and viewer
  grids) that lit a flipped cell. The test is exact, not heuristic --
  castFovOctant reads exactly the cells it lights -- and a viewer whose cast
  never reached the diamonds keeps its cache, where upstream recomputes the
  identical grid (its lone caller discards the recomputed bool, so the
  packets are byte-identical).

Third pass -- per-connection work whose output is identical for every
connection, and per-object wire encoding:

- loadRgbaSprite: each of the 16 connections re-read and re-decoded the same
  pickup PNG from disk (~10 ms) the first time it saw each sprite family;
  memoized per (name, size, alphaCutoff).
- the init snapshot: buildSpriteProtocolPlayerInit names no player, yet every
  connection rebuilt it (~58 ms each: map raster + compression, flag
  sprites, the soldier pool). One build per (gameId, tick) is cached; the 15
  sibling connections of an episode copy it, defs included.
- cosmetic raster builders (hp bar, identity badge, splatter, hit spark,
  tracer dot/head, floating pop): pure functions of small keys,
  re-rasterized per connection per def -- the tracer pair even eagerly, as
  call arguments the def dedup then discarded; memoized process-wide behind
  one shared `memoized` template.
- addBoardObject: the addObject path is six cross-module calls with a setLen
  each, paid for every object every frame; encoded in place as one setLen
  plus twelve byte stores.
- fog runs: the emit loop re-encoded ~150 unchanged objects per viewer per
  frame; the object block is now cached next to the run list and re-appended
  as one memcpy (def sends tracked per connection in fogWidthsSeen). The
  rebuild scan reads 8 cells per word where runs are uniform.
- fog cone: vx/vy/d2/sqrt(d2) are pure functions of the cell offset from the
  viewer, tabulated once per grid size; the pass over a fresh shadow copy
  walks the shadow's lit-cell list (kept beside the per-cell shadow cache,
  on the SimServer so it is per-game by construction) instead of sweeping
  all ~12.9k cells.
- HUD text (lives, weapon, team scoreboard): the label carries the whole
  text, so a def already holding it means addSpriteChanged would drop the
  send; the glyphs are now rasterized only when the label differs, widths
  read off the def.
- object-delete sweep again: the per-frame HashSet allocated and re-hashed
  ~300 ids; replaced with a generation stamp per object id.

Fourth pass -- what the GV30 rebase made expensive, and still is:

- painted diamonds: a diamond that has been shot re-ships its repainted
  stone to every viewer on every spin advance, and each viewer rasterized
  the paint (buildPaintStainSprite per stain, composited per pixel) plus the
  base-frame copy itself -- ~680 ms of a 1.28 s trace across
  buildPaintStainSprite / buildPaintedDiamondPixels / addRotatingDiamonds.
  The raster is pure in (this diamond's stain list, frame, boardScale), and
  diamondStains is APPEND-ONLY within a match, so within one (gameId,
  stainEpoch) its length pins its contents: memoized per (diamond, frame,
  paintCount, boardScale), cache dropped whenever the stamp changes.
  stainEpoch is a SimServer field bumped exactly where the stain lists
  reset (startGame / resetToLobby); it is never hashed.
- fov invalidation cadence: the spin advances every 4 ticks and upstream
  invalidates every viewer's fov cache each time; the exact flipped-cell
  sweep above cuts the forced shadowcasts by the share of viewers whose
  vision never reached the changed stone (~11% on this arena -- the
  diamonds sit mid-map, so most casts do reach them; on maps with more
  occlusion the share grows).

Fifth pass -- headless observation. The first four passes made the engine
compute its pixels faster; this one stops computing most of them, which is
the larger idea and the one the earlier passes kept walking past.

The policy reads a CLOSED VOCABULARY off the wire (bot/baseline/labelkind.nim
resolves every label to a kind, and `lkOther` -- most of a frame -- is
dropped from the frame index before anything looks at it). Sprite PIXELS it
never reads at all, save the walkability map. Meanwhile addSpriteChanged has
always deduped on metadata (width, height, label, the caller's `changed`) and
never on pixel content, so nothing downstream of a raster depends on what is
in it. Put those together and the fog overlay, the spinning stone, the
splatters and the floating damage numbers are computed, upscaled, compressed
and shipped for a reader that discards them on arrival.

- spriteObservedHook: a nil-by-default hook naming the labels a reader can
  use. Off the hook -- every hosted server, every replay, every test -- this
  patch changes nothing at all. `sim/simulate.nim` installs the union of both
  policy trees' `classify(label) != lkOther`, so the engine's idea of what is
  worth building IS the consumer's idea of what is worth reading, at its
  source: a policy that adds a `classify` arm gets that family back with no
  engine change, and no hand-kept list can go stale against it.
- addSpriteChanged / addBoardSpriteChanged: the definition of an unobserved
  sprite is not sent, and `spriteDefs` is updated exactly as if it had been,
  so every dedup, every knownTextDefWidth and every def-exists gate branches
  identically. The board path tests before `scaleSpritePixels`, which is the
  single largest piece of work this drops.
- the init snapshot's map raster: buildSpriteProtocolPlayerInit copied the
  whole baked arena (1235x659 RGBA, ~3.3 MB) for a definition the gate above
  then dropped. Its only consumer is that send, so it is built only if the
  send survives.

`spriteObserved` is deliberately NOT memoized, which is worth stating because
the arithmetic argues the other way: it is on the emit path for every sprite
of every viewer of every frame, and a label's answer never changes. A
`Table[string, bool]` in front of it measured SLOWER on the six seeds (0.72
-> 0.76 ms/tick, both halves of a back-to-back pair) -- a hash probe hashes
the string and compares it on a hit, which is no cheaper than the
length-and-hash switch Nim compiles `classify`'s `case` into. Keying on the
sprite id would be cheaper still and is unavailable: one painted-diamond id
carries "diamond 3 paint 1", "diamond 3 paint 2" and so on, so an id does not
determine a label.
- addRotatingDiamonds asks BEFORE it rasterizes, so the dearest raster in
  the build -- a stain composited per pixel, per stain, per spin step -- is
  not built either. It asks with `labelDiamondPainted(...)` / `LabelDiamond`,
  the same expressions the send uses, so the test cannot drift from what it
  guards; and it gates the RASTER ONLY, placing its object either way (see
  below). The splatter and damage-pop emitters deliberately do NOT ask: their
  rasters are already memoized, so a check would buy a cache lookup and cost
  the invariant below.
- addFogRuns skips the whole overlay, objects included. That is a stronger
  skip, sound only because `LabelFog` is the ONLY label it emits, so the
  decision is all-or-nothing; it is also the largest single saving here
  (~150 object messages per viewer per frame, plus the run-list rebuild and
  the client's decode of all of it). It leaves refreshPlayerFov alone: the
  visibility grid still decides what is visible and every entity is still
  culled against it, only the darkness drawn over the rest goes.

Why this is not a change to what the policy sees: a suppressed definition
leaves the client's sprite table without an entry, and `refreshFrame` already
drops an object whose sprite is undefined -- the same drop it applies to a
label it does not scan. The objects are still placed either way. So the frame
index the policy reads is identical, and the six-seed gameHash comparison
says so.

That last invariant -- THE OBJECT STREAM IS NOT A FUNCTION OF THE PREDICATE
-- is the one to defend when editing this. An emitter that skipped a whole
item would skip its `currentIds.add` and its pool-slot counter too, which
renumbers every later item into a different object id and makes the delete
sweep ship removals. No gameHash run can see that while a family is
uniformly unread, and it stops being uniformly unread the moment a policy
adds one `classify` arm: read the clean stone but not `diamond 3 paint 2`
and a static obstacle disappears mid-episode, silently. An earlier draft of
this pass had exactly that shape in four emitters. addFogRuns is the sole
exception and earns it structurally, on the one-label argument above.

Verifying it after a pin move (this is the check that matters, and it is
mechanical): build with -d:dumpLabels, which records every label that reaches
the wire and writes it to $LABEL_DUMP at exit. Run once with the hook
installed and once without it (comment out the assignment in simulate.nim);
every label in the difference must be one `classify` maps to lkOther, and
nothing may appear only in the hooked run. At this pin the difference is
exactly (573 labels down to 246 on seed 5000): Room *, blast stage *, <color>
flag carrier glow, corpse *, damage pop *, diamond *, endzone * column *,
fire icon cooldown, fog, game teams * map *, grenade sound, hit splat *,
interstitial background, map, own aim *, shield bubble, shield bubble hit,
splatter *, spray paint puff, weapon *.

Five of those are new since the GV30 patch and all five are `lkOther` for
this policy: `endzone * column *`, `game teams * map *` and `own aim *` are
contract markers the bot has no `classify` arm for, `shield bubble hit` and
`spray paint puff` are chrome. (`blast` is the same family, renamed to
`blast stage *`.) That is the mechanism working rather than a gap -- adding
the arm turns the family's emission back on with no engine change -- but
`own aim ` is the one worth knowing about, because docs/PROTOCOL.md says a
policy that dead-reckons its aim open-loop measurably loses accuracy.
Note `map` too: the policy needs the map OBJECT for its camera, but that is
keyed off (objectId, spriteId) in the object message and never touches the
definition, so the definition -- a compressed 1235x659 raster -- goes.

Also in this pass, on the setup rather than the frame:

- the map bake (MapBake in sim.nim): loadMapLayers plus the three per-pixel
  passes over the board plus buildFovBlocked, about 430 ms, are a pure
  function of the resolved CtfMap and were paid once per EPISODE. Cached on
  that map by value, so a worker running a batch of seeds bakes the arena
  once and a pool/gen draw onto different terrain rebuilds. Fonts, palette,
  sprite sheet and crew sprites are files on disk and are loaded once per
  process. Marginal setup per episode: ~1.16 s -> ~0.05 s. This is what makes
  scripts/local_sim.py's batching worth doing; its `selfcheck` step pins that
  a batched episode is the episode it would have been alone.

Sixth pass -- the fog stops materializing a grid nobody reads, and the
spinning stone stops being rasterized for angles it has already been at.

The fifth pass stopped BUILDING pixels nobody looks at. The first of these
is the same idea one level down, on the thing that pass walked past:
refreshPlayerFov still materialized a whole 12.9k-cell visibility GRID per
viewer per frame -- a copy of the cached shadowcast, then a cone test on
every lit cell in it -- and the only consumer that wants a grid is the fog
overlay, which the fifth pass had already switched off. Everything else asks
`fovVisibleAt` about a POINT, a few dozen times a frame.

- FovCone: what the cone loop used to hoist out of itself (the aim vector,
  the cone cosine, the bubble radius, the GV34 range cap, the origin's
  shifts into the offset tables), resolved once per refresh and kept on the
  PlayerFov.
- keeps(cone, cell): THE cone expression. The grid pass and the point query
  both call it, so the two cannot drift by an ulp -- and an ulp here is one
  flipped cell, which is a different episode. It runs bubble, then range,
  then cone, which is upstream's order and matters: a cell inside the bubble
  survives a range cap it is not subject to.
- refreshPlayerFov updates the shadow and the cone and stops.
  ensureFovVisible builds the grid; addFogRuns calls it and nothing else
  does.
- fovVisibleAt is `shadow[cell] and cone.keeps(cell)`: the same two
  conditions, in the same order, that the grid pass would have written into
  that cell.
- FovShadow.lit is built on demand too (ensureLit). It exists only for the
  grid pass, so a headless run never pays the 12.9k-cell scan that fills it.

Off the hook -- every hosted server, every replay, every test -- addFogRuns
runs on every frame, so the grid is built exactly when it always was, out of
the same expression, and the wire is unchanged. On the hook it is built
never.

The second was invisible to a {.measure.} profile and came out of callgrind,
which counts what is NOT marked: the diamond restamp -- stampDiamondPatch,
rotatedDiamondCovers and refreshFovCells together -- was 20% of a tick's
instructions, the largest single block in the engine's share and larger than
the shadowcast. A spin step rasterized ~4k pixels of trigonometry per window
and then re-derived the fog occlusion of ~640 cells from 41k pixel reads,
every four ticks, forever.

- The stone is a pure function of (baseWall, the diamonds sharing the
  window, their frames), and a diamond only ever visits DiamondSpinFrames
  angles: cached per (window, frame vector) as the two masks it writes, so a
  match rasterizes each window sixteen times over its whole length instead
  of every four ticks. What is left of a restamp is `h` pairs of memcpy --
  the same bytes in the same places, `base OR stone` still, just not
  recomputed to find out what it was. The frame vector folds into an int64
  key, and a window shared by more than DiamondStampGroupMax diamonds is
  computed uncached rather than risking a key collision, which here would
  mean laying one angle's stone for another's. That bound is a fact about
  upstream's DiamondSpinFrames, so a `static:` block asserts the fold cannot
  overflow -- a pin that widens the spin fails the BUILD rather than the
  episode.
- refreshFovCells counts its wall pixels eight at a time: a bool is exactly
  0 or 1, so `wall and not window` over a uint64 and a multiply by
  0x01..01 sums the eight bytes into the top one. Same count, same pixels.

Not done, and recorded so it is not tried twice: keying the shadowcast cache
on the diamond ANGLE as well as the origin cell, so a turn made an entry
inapplicable rather than stale and a cast came back when the stone came back
round. It measured SLOWER (1.035 -> 1.065 ms/tick). The reuse is not there
to collect -- viewers keep walking into cells nobody has stood in at this
angle -- so the wider key and the memory bound are paid for nothing. What it
was chasing is real: never invalidating at all is worth ~12%, and is of
course wrong. That is the size of the prize and the reason it stays
unclaimed.

Numbers on THIS pin and this machine (a 3-core box, otherwise idle; every
figure below is one box's, so quote the ratio and never the column). Six
reference episodes, seeds 5000-5005, 23233 game ticks, one worker, no
compile, runs alternated:

    unpatched  110.3 s   4.748 ms/tick
    patched     10.7 s   0.463 ms/tick     10.3x

End to end through scripts/local_sim.py -- `h2h HEAD HEAD -n 8`, 16 episodes
both directions, 62396 ticks, two workers, COLD build both (switching
CTF_ENGINE_DIR invalidates build.sh's stamp, so neither run got to keep a
nimcache the other did not):

    unpatched  2 m 49 s    ~340 episodes/hour
    patched      36 s     ~1600 episodes/hour     4.7x

All six reference gameHashes are identical patched and unpatched, and so are
all sixteen of that head-to-head's. That last clause is the one worth
re-running after any change here: it says the simulator reproduces the
unmodified upstream engine exactly, which is the only reason any of this is
allowed to be fast. (The stock build is also the check that
`sim/simulate.nim` still compiles without the hook -- a CTF_ENGINE_DIR
checkout is never patched, and it is supposed to keep working, just slowly.)
The patched engine also passes coworld-ctf's own suite (`nim c -r -d:release
tests/tests.nim` from the .engine root, 401 checks), which is what covers the
paths the hook turns off and a headless run therefore never takes.

A SEVENTH PASS HAS SOMEWHERE OBVIOUS TO START, and the rebase is what put it
there. GV34 capped the vision cone at 1.5x gun range, which means a cell
outside that radius is dark for every aim -- so the shadowcast still walks to
the map edge to light cells the cone then throws away, and the per-cell
shadow cache stores them. Bounding castFovOctant's row walk by the range cap
would be the same episode by construction only if the bound is derived from
the same rangeSq the cone tests, which is exactly the ulp-boundary trap the
sixth pass's note warns about; do it by comparing against a CELL-space bound
that is provably conservative, and check it the usual way.

