+when defined(dumpLabels):
+  import std/sets
+  var labelVocab*: HashSet[string]
+  proc dumpLabelVocab*() =
+    ## Every label addSpriteChanged was asked for, written at exit. This is
+    ## what proves an elided family is one no policy reads: dump the
+    ## vocabulary with the hook OFF, classify each label with the policy's
+    ## own `classify`, and check the elided ones all land on `lkOther`.
+    ## Off by default; `sim/build.sh ... -d:dumpLabels` turns it on.
+    var lines: seq[string]
+    for l in labelVocab: lines.add l
+    lines.sort()
+    writeFile(getEnv("LABEL_DUMP", "/tmp/labels.txt"), lines.join("\n") & "\n")
+
+const NoPixels: array[0, uint8] = []
+  ## The pixels of a sprite nobody looks at.
+
+var spriteObservedHook*: proc(label: string): bool = nil
+  ## Installed by a HEADLESS host — the local simulator — to say which sprite
+  ## labels its policy can actually read. Nil by default, so a hosted server,
+  ## a replay and every test emit exactly what they always did: this whole
+  ## mechanism is inert until something opts in.
+  ##
+  ## Why a hook rather than a list here: the answer belongs to the policy,
+  ## not to the engine. `sim/simulate.nim` installs the union of both builds'
+  ## `labelkind.classify(label) != lkOther`, which IS the consumer's
+  ## vocabulary at its source — so a policy that starts reading a family
+  ## turns that family's emission back on with no engine change, and cannot
+  ## be starved by a stale list.
+  ##
+  ## What suppression means, precisely: the sprite DEFINITION is not shipped,
+  ## while `spriteDefs` is updated exactly as if it had been. Objects that
+  ## reference it are still placed, and the client drops them for the sprite
+  ## being undefined — the same drop it already applies to a label it does
+  ## not scan. So the observation the policy reads is unchanged, and the
+  ## pixels, the compression and (for the emitters that check first) the
+  ## rasterization are all skipped.
+  ##
+  ## THE OBJECT STREAM IS NOT A FUNCTION OF THIS PREDICATE, and must not
+  ## become one. An emitter that skipped a whole item would also skip its
+  ## `currentIds.add` and its pool-slot counter, which renumbers every later
+  ## item into a different object id and makes the delete sweep ship removals
+  ## — a difference no `gameHash` run can see while a family is uniformly
+  ## unread, and a static obstacle vanishing mid-episode the first time one
+  ## is not. Emitters that test this predicate therefore gate the RASTER and
+  ## place the object regardless. `addFogRuns` is the single exception and
+  ## says why at its own definition: it emits exactly one label, so its skip
+  ## cannot be partial.
+
+proc spriteObserved*(label: string): bool {.inline.} =
+  ## Whether any policy on this wire reads `label`. Always true off the hook.
+  ##
+  ## Deliberately NOT memoized, though it sits on the emit path for every
+  ## sprite of every viewer of every frame and the answer for a given label
+  ## never changes. A `Table[string, bool]` in front of it looks free on an
+  ## instruction count and MEASURED SLOWER on the six reference seeds --
+  ## 0.72 -> 0.76 ms/tick, both halves of a back-to-back pair. A hash probe
+  ## hashes the string and then compares it on a hit, which is no cheaper
+  ## than the length-and-hash switch Nim compiles `classify`'s `case` into,
+  ## and it trades a branchy cache-warm path for a random one.
